    {
      "id": "p0176-b008",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "source": {
        "text": "This is a humorous reference to a famous food in Japan called daigaku imo, which translates as \"college potato\" or \"university potato\". Daigaku imo is basically deep-fried sweet potato covered with a hard sugar coating. It first gained popularity in the 1900s as an inexpensive, but filling meal for Japanese university students, and has since become a standard dish that can be found in countless recipe books, restaurants, and food stalls throughout Japan.",
        "raw": "This is a humorous reference to a famous food in Japan called daigaku imo,\nwhich translates as \"college potato\" or \"university potato\". Daigaku imo is\nbasically deep-fried sweet potato covered with a hard sugar coating. It first\ngained popularity in the 1900s as an inexpensive, but filling meal for Japanese\nuniversity students, and has since become a standard dish that can be found in\ncountless recipe books, restaurants, and food stalls throughout Japan.",
        "status": "corrected",
        "languages": [
          "en"
        ],
        "bbox": [
          0.29394631,
          0.54384286,
          0.89297927,
          0.64735714
        ],
        "mask_bbox": [
          0.29095247,
          0.54312857,
          0.89823377,
          0.64842857
        ],
        "line_ids": [
          28,
          29,
          30,
          31,
          32,
          33
        ]
      },
      "translation": {
        "text": "这句打趣的是日本名吃“大学芋”（daigaku imo），字面意思就是“大学的薯”。它是炸过后裹上硬糖衣的红薯。20 世纪初，这种便宜又顶饱的食物在日本大学生中流行起来，此后成为常见小吃，日本各地无数食谱、餐馆和摊位上都能见到。",
        "status": "draft",
